---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-brevo';
const title = 'Postscript vs Brevo for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Compare Postscript and Brevo for a cross-border Shopify store: channel migration, destination-specific SMS setup, purchase cancellation and matched costs.';

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Postscript or Brevo better for Shopify SMS?",
    "answer": "Choose against the destinations and journeys your store needs. Postscript now offers Email as well as SMS. Brevo documents SMS campaigns and automation actions, with country-specific sender requirements. Neither a channel label nor a geographic headquarters establishes a winner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Brevo replace Postscript for a Shopify store?",
    "answer": "Potentially, if the required destinations, sender registration, Shopify events and recovery logic work in the proposed Brevo account. Reproduce your current campaigns and cancellation behavior before retiring Postscript; there is no defensible monthly-message cutoff for this decision."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I use Brevo for email and Postscript for SMS?",
    "answer": "This can preserve an established Brevo email program while retaining Postscript SMS. Assign an owner for each journey and propagate purchase and suppression changes before the next eligible message. Also evaluate Postscript Email rather than assuming two suppliers are necessary."
  },
  {
    "question": "How does Postscript vs Brevo pricing compare?",
    "answer": "Compare written quotes for the same destinations, message lengths, email volume and plan capabilities. Brevo documents SMS credits and destination-dependent costs. Include registration, carrier charges where applicable, migration work and temporary overlap; a starting price cannot establish the cheaper stack."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Brevo or Postscript?",
    "answer": "Consider Sequenzy as a separate email candidate if the email workflow is the problem. Demonstrate the required Shopify events, templates and suppression behavior, and obtain a quote. Adding a third supplier needs a concrete benefit that exceeds the added coordination work."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which is better for GDPR and EU Shopify merchants?",
    "answer": "Begin with the actual recipient countries, processing terms and purpose of each message. Brevo publishes country-specific SMS restrictions and sender registration guidance. Neither supplier selection nor an EU address alone establishes lawful processing or permission to send."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which handles cart abandonment better?",
    "answer": "Use a proposed cart test with an eligible subscriber, a purchase before the scheduled text, a duplicate event and a reply. Compare product context and cancellation behavior in each configured account. No measured recovery advantage is claimed here."
  },
  {
    "question": "What workflow test should decide the winner?",
    "answer": "For a cross-border store, test the same cart journey in each intended destination, including sender approval, separate channel permissions, purchase cancellation and reply ownership. Reject a configuration that cannot meet a required destination or handoff before comparing commercial terms."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Postscript vs Brevo</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs Brevo for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">A Shopify store selling across borders needs a workable sender and journey in each market. Compare keeping Brevo email alongside Postscript SMS with moving both channels to one supplier. Postscript also offers Email, so consolidation is a choice in both directions.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">The cross-border launch below is a proposed evaluation, not a merchant case study or an observed conversion result.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Existing Brevo email', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Preserve working email journeys while testing the required SMS destinations.' },
            { label: 'Existing Postscript SMS', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Evaluate Email migration alongside the SMS program already in use.' },
            { label: 'Split channels', tool: 'Either stack', reason: 'Retain a split only with explicit purchase, suppression and queue ownership.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Postscript</th><th>Brevo</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Channel scope</td><td>Email and SMS offering</td><td>Email and SMS campaigns and automation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cross-border acceptance</td><td>Confirm destination and sender eligibility for the proposed account</td><td>Country-specific restrictions and sender registration documented</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Migration direction</td><td>Test moving email beside an existing SMS program</td><td>Test moving SMS beside an existing email program</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cart cancellation</td><td>Demonstrate purchase exits before a queued send</td><td>Demonstrate purchase exits before a queued send</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cost comparison</td><td>Quote the full email and SMS scope</td><td>Quote email scope and destination-specific SMS usage</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the market that could block the launch</h2>
        <p>Consider a proposed skincare store with an established European email audience and a new US product launch. The question is whether to extend Brevo into the launch SMS program, retain Postscript for that program, or consolidate email into Postscript. Existing email templates are an asset to preserve, not proof that the email supplier should own every text.</p>
        <p>Make a destination matrix before building campaigns: recipient country, sender type, registration status, message purpose and operational owner. A country code is a routing input, not proof of residence or permission. Keep email and SMS permission evidence separate even when both identifiers resolve to one Shopify customer. Do not infer promotional permission from an order or an imported phone field.</p>

        <h2>What the current product documentation establishes</h2>
        <p>Postscript’s <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/launch">product releases</a> include Email. Email migration therefore belongs in this comparison; the old claim that it is unavailable would exclude a valid option.</p>
        <p>Brevo documents an <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424283046418-Send-SMS-messages-through-an-automation">SMS automation action</a> and <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/11884482521234-Country-specific-guidelines-and-restrictions-for-SMS-messages">country-specific restrictions</a>. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/28255350696466-Register-a-Sender-ID-to-send-SMS-messages">sender registration guide</a> calls for a registered toll-free number for US and Canadian SMS. That is a concrete setup dependency, not evidence that either supplier produces better legal or revenue outcomes.</p>
        <p>An SMS action does not by itself establish that your configured Shopify connector supplies every cart field or cancellation event. Ask for a demonstration using the actual integration and plan: product variant, currency, checkout link, subscriber state and purchase timestamp. Record missing fields before rebuilding a live journey.</p>

        <h2>A proposed launch rehearsal across two destinations</h2>
        <p>Use authorized test identities representing the intended European and US destinations. Start with email permission only, then add SMS permission through the intended enrollment surface. Confirm that the first state remains ineligible for promotional text. Record the disclosure shown, source, timestamp and destination alongside the later consent change.</p>
        <p>Prepare a launch email and a follow-up text for subscribers who remain eligible. Confirm that each destination has an approved sender and that local scheduling behaves as intended. A sender approved in one destination is not a substitute for completing another destination’s requirements. If the destination cannot be served, exclude it explicitly rather than silently substituting another sender.</p>
        <p>Repeat with a shopper purchasing before the text is due and another withdrawing SMS permission after joining the queue. Inspect the pending action and the final send decision. The expected result is cancellation where the journey or permission requires it. These are acceptance criteria, not claims that either platform has passed this rehearsal.</p>

        <h2>Keep a split stack only with a workable handoff</h2>
        <p>If Brevo retains email and Postscript retains SMS, name the sender for each launch and cart touch. Decide which system consumes the purchase event and how the other learns the journey has ended. Check the latest purchase and channel suppression state before sending; a stale segment export can schedule a text after the email journey has already exited.</p>
        <p>Replay the same cart event and check that it does not create a second recovery sequence. Then test a repeat customer whose email address changed while the phone number stayed the same. Identity matching should preserve evidence and current channel state rather than treating every new identifier as a new acquisition.</p>
        <p>For replies, route a product-use question to a named support owner with the relevant order or product context. Demonstrate the actual number and inbox configuration on each candidate. A documented campaign action alone does not prove an operational conversation handoff. Define who pauses promotional follow-up when a shopper is waiting for help.</p>

        <h2>Price the destination mix and migration work</h2>
        <p>Brevo’s <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/208717449-Supported-countries-and-pricing-for-SMS-messages">SMS country and pricing guidance</a> makes destination part of the cost comparison. Give both suppliers the same expected destination mix, message lengths, email sends and peak campaign schedule. Include SMS credits or usage, plan entitlements, sender setup, applicable carrier charges and staff time. Confirm how long copy, Unicode and MMS affect the quoted usage.</p>
        <p>Compare steady operation with the transition period separately. Moving email entails template work, domain configuration, suppression migration and validation of existing journeys. Moving SMS entails sender and enrollment continuity as well as flow recreation. Temporary dual billing may be reasonable, but it belongs in the decision rather than disappearing behind a low entry price.</p>
        <p>Evaluate contribution after message costs, discounts, returns and fulfillment costs. Platform-attributed orders are useful for reconciling reports, but do not establish incremental orders. If the business later runs an incrementality experiment, define the eligible audience and holdout before launch; this page reports no experiment or recovered-revenue result.</p>

        <h2>Where Sequenzy belongs in the decision</h2>
        <p>Sequenzy can remain an email candidate if rebuilding email is already in scope. Give it the same product-education and repeat-purchase journey to demonstrate, including the Shopify event source and purchase exit. Do not add it simply because the text channel needs a supplier, or assume it delivers lower costs or better strategy without the relevant workflow and quote.</p>

        <h2>Make the choice around the failed or preserved job</h2>
        <p>Extending Brevo is useful if it preserves a working email program and meets the launch’s destination, event and handoff requirements. Retaining Postscript SMS is useful if replacing that configured program introduces more work than it removes. Moving email to Postscript is a separate consolidation path to demonstrate. Choose after documenting the work each option preserves and the required steps it cannot perform; geography, message volume and vendor count alone do not decide it.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-mailchimp">Brevo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-omnisend">Brevo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp">Postscript vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/beauty">Email apps for beauty stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs Brevo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
